WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
Data and privacy terms are part of the choice.

2
00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:07.747
For many teams, the deciding factor is not capability but where data goes.

3
00:00:08.027 --> 00:00:16.133
Read each provider’s terms for the exact surface you will use (API, consumer app, or a cloud platform), because they differ.

4
00:00:16.413 --> 00:00:33.309
OpenAI states that data sent to its API is not used to train its models unless you opt in, that abuse-monitoring logs are kept for up to 30 days by default, and that zero data retention requires OpenAI’s approval and covers only some endpoints.

5
00:00:33.589 --> 00:00:44.085
Google’s Gemini API terms distinguish unpaid services (prompts and responses may be used to improve Google’s products and may be read by human reviewers,

6
00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:54.499
so the terms advise against sending sensitive data) from paid services (prompts are not used to improve products and are logged for a limited time for abuse detection).

7
00:00:54.779 --> 00:01:14.192
xAI’s API security FAQ says requests and responses are retained for 30 days for abuse auditing, are not used for training without explicit permission, and that a team admin can enable zero data retention, which disables features that depend on stored data such as batch and file storage.

8
00:01:14.472 --> 00:01:24.605
Anthropic documents its API retention and zero data retention arrangements in its data retention docs; Studio 6 covers them alongside the others.

9
00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:35.381
Open weights keep inference data entirely inside your systems, which is why they are often the only option for regulated or contractual “no third party” data.

10
00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:37.816
Two traps from Studio 6 apply here.

11
00:01:38.096 --> 00:01:44.197
Provider zero data retention says nothing about your own logs, traces, and eval datasets.

12
00:01:44.477 --> 00:01:50.856
And the consumer app’s terms are not the API’s terms; a policy written for one does not cover the other.
